# TwinCAT ST — Pagefault Safety (InfoSys-only)

Use for page faults, access violations, null POINTER/REFERENCE/INTERFACE,
`AND` vs `AND_THEN`, `__NEW`/`__DELETE`, MEMCPY size, array bounds, or
`/twincat3-cmd-pagefault-audit`.

Check-IDs: skill `twincat3-pagefault-audit` → `checklist.md` + `infosys-evidence.md`.

## Hard rules (only MSHC-proven)

| Topic | Required |
|-------|----------|
| `POINTER TO` | `p <> 0` before every `p^` / call through pointer (SA0039) |
| `REFERENCE TO` | `__ISVALIDREF(ref)` before use |
| Interface | `i <> 0` before method/property — **never** `__ISVALIDREF` |
| `AND` / `OR` | Never use IEC `AND`/`OR` to guard a deref/index/call/division — always evaluates further operands; use nested `IF` or `AND_THEN`/`OR_ELSE` — **also in `WHILE`/`REPEAT` conditions** |
| `__NEW` | Check result `<> 0` before use |
| `__DELETE` | No use of operand or aliases after delete |
| `MEMCPY`/`MEMMOVE`/`MEMSET` | Byte count `n` must not exceed real buffer (crash class) |
| Arrays | Variable index within **declared** bounds |
| Division | Divisor `<> 0` before `/`/`DIV` (InfoSys: exception → **task stop**; IDs `EX-DIV-*`, not pagefault) |

## Do not report as pagefault (out of scope)

- CONCAT / string truncation
- MEMCPY null dest/src alone (returns 0)
- Memory leak without `__DELETE` (unstable/leak ≠ pagefault ID)
- Speculative lifetime / Online-Change / JSON toolkit IDs not in checklist
- Treating `VAR_IN_OUT` as optional/invalid (InfoSys: always assigned)

